O and Q are much alike, except for a squiggle;
to think one tiny stroke has made the whole world wriggle.

_Reference translation by Taufiq Rafat, "Bulleh Shah: A Selection" (Vanguard, 1982), p.37. COPYRIGHTED — private reference only; do NOT publish or use for training. Rafat maps the Urdu letters ain/ghain to English O/Q._

The form of 'ain is just like ghain;
a single dot sets them apart — yet over that one dot the bewildered world keeps wandering.

_AI-drafted (Claude) from the Urdu original; needs review._

Robbers lodge in the temple rest-house, cheats at the idol's shrine-gate;
and inside the mosque too they take their seats — only the lover keeps apart.

_AI-drafted (Claude) from the Urdu original; needs review._

You stay up all night to pray,
but dogs are sleepless too;
    they're better than you.

To bark and bark, and then to sleep
on the first trash-heap you view;
    they're better than you.

They will not leave their master's house
though beaten black and blue;
    they're better than you.

Bulleh, you'd better mend your ways,
else it will be proved true:
    dogs are better than you.

_Reference translation by Taufiq Rafat, "Bulleh Shah: A Selection" (Vanguard, 1982), p.125. COPYRIGHTED — private reference only; do NOT publish or use for training._

Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

Your mother counsels you every day, daughter!
Why do you drift about so aimlessly, daughter!
Do not throw away your modesty, daughter —
when will you ever understand, foolish girl?
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

Daily I give my counsel to her,
to this simple, dazed, half-mad girl;
hard times will fall upon the lonely one —
only then, poor thing, will she realize, girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

Today there is fresh cotton in the house, girl;
quickly set up the cotton-gin, girl;
gin it, then go and card it, girl,
or you will have no rest tomorrow, girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

This reign in your parents' home is but four days, girl;
do not fritter it away in play, girl;
do not sit idle — get to your work, girl;
do not let your household fall to ruin, girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

You will not stay in your parents' home forever;
you will not always sit beside your mother;
foolish one, in the end you must bear the parting;
then you will be left helpless, girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

Spin, understand it, get it spun;
now set up the warp and the thread;
get your own dowry dyed, girl;
keep your yarn with care, girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

Take no pride in beauty and youth;
nor in the roaming of a far-off land;
this world is false and perishable —
not a name, not a trace of it will remain, girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

A hard hour is going to come;
all your kin and friends will flee;
the one who will help and ferry you across —
that is Bulleh's Sultan (his master), girl.
Turn your mind to your spinning, girl.

_AI-drafted (Claude) from the Urdu original; needs review._

Someone ask: what does the Beloved do? — Whatever He does, so He does!

In the mosque He offers the prayer, then goes and enters the idol-temple;
He alone is one, yet the owner of countless houses, master of every home;
dwelling close within one house, no veil is left between;
wherever I turn my gaze, there is He — He keeps company with all;
within the river of Oneness He ferries the whole world across.
Bulleh — the Beloved's love is a leech: it drinks the blood, it seizes the flesh.
Someone ask: what does the Beloved do? — "Whatever He does, so He does!"

_AI-drafted (Claude) from the Urdu original; needs review._

What kind of repentance is this repentance? — do not repent like this, friend.

Repentance by the mouth, not from the heart; from such repentance he renounces nothing;
what heedlessness has drawn its veil over you — why should the Forgiver forgive you?

[Giving little and taking more, staking everything on a gamble];
how would he ever attain true Islam, whose conduct is such as this?

[You go where you should not, you take a stranger's blows];
you set the holy books on your head [and swear] — yet what trust can be placed in you?

The tyrants have no fear of their tyranny; they die by their own doings;
they hold no fear of God — here and hereafter they are brought to disgrace.

What kind of repentance is this repentance? — do not repent like this, friend.

_AI-drafted (Claude) from the Urdu original; needs review._
